loadkeys: a catalogue keymap becomes the system's keyboard setting.

The keyboard layout of the system is /etc/likeos/keyboard.conf, shared by
the console and the X session; the layouts are the lines of
/usr/share/likeos/keyboard/layouts.tsv, and /usr/share/keymaps/<id>.map is
the console keymap of catalogue entry <id>.  So that "loadkeys de" changes
the layout of the whole system rather than of the console alone:

 * After loading one keymap of /usr/share/keymaps whose name (through an
   alias link, e.g. de-latin1.map -> de.map) is a catalogue id, loadkeys
   runs "likeos-keyboard-save --console <id>"; the setuid helper records
   the layout and the X session follows.  A missing or failing helper is a
   warning, the keymap stays loaded.

 * A process that may not load a keymap (an ordinary user in a terminal
   window: the console is not its terminal) hands a catalogue layout to
   the helper's other form, "likeos-keyboard-save LAYOUT VARIANT OPTIONS",
   which records it and loads the keymap as root.  The current OPTIONS are
   kept.

 * --no-save turns both off; the helper and the boot use it.

src/likeos-keyboard.c holds the LikeOS part; loadkeys.c only calls it, and
the keymap search path is now set before the console is opened so that the
keymap can be identified first.

